WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:03.200
I’m often asked
what my opinion

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.760
about a diet
or disease is.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:12.080
Who cares what my or
anyone else’s opinion is.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:16.160
All we should care about
is what the science says.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.240
What does the best available
balance of evidence

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.240
Published in the peer-
reviewed medical literature

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:21.680
have to say right now?

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.960
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.520
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.360
I know so many people

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.720
who have been diagnosed
with Alzheimer’s disease,

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:34.960
the most common
cause of dementia.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.040
And I bet you do too.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.480
In fact, more than six million
people in the US have it.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.880
So how do we avoid becoming
one of those statistics?

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.920
That's a main thrust of
my forthcoming book,

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:49.405
How Not to Age.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:50.720
But in the meanwhile,

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:54.080
here's a discussion of some
little-known risk factors in this,

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.840
our 300th podcast episode.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.440
In our first story, we explore the effect
of pesticides on Alzheimer’s disease.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:07.280
Although there is a growing list
of Alzheimer's disease

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:10.400
susceptibility genes, even if
you put them all altogether,

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.200
they account for less than half
of all Alzheimer's cases.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.800
The single most compelling piece
of data on the potential control

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.360
we have over the disease
is the fact that if you have

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.720
identical twins, with the exact
same genes, even if one gets

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:25.760
Alzheimer's the other
usually does not.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:27.360
So, we have to think
about all the other

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.840
contributing factors
beyond just genetics.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:34.720
In my video on pesticides and cancer,
I talked about this study.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:38.160
There’s a list of chlorinated
pesticides, including DDE

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.920
(a metabolite of DDT) that
are classified by the EPA

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.160
as probable human carcinogens.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:47.200
But in the study, blood
levels of DDE and others were

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:50.160
associated not with
increased cancer mortality,

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.800
but increased risk of
other-cause mortality.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.600
This led researchers to
speculate it may be due to

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.000
an associated increased risk
of diabetes or dementia.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.640
I’ve talked previously
about the diabetes link.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.000
What about dementia?

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:08.880
“Elevated Serum Pesticide Levels
and Risk for Alzheimer Disease.”

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:13.280
A research team at Rutgers found
significantly higher blood

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:17.680
levels of DDE in Alzheimer’s disease
patients compared to controls,

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:19.440
and autopsy studies
show blood levels

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.840
are a good proxy for brain levels.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.560
Those with the highest levels
were at about four times

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:26.960
the odds of being
demented with Alzheimer's.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.800
And in a petri dish, DDE increases
amyloid precursor protein levels

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.160
in human brain cells, providing
a potential mechanism.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.360
Put all these studies together,
and there does indeed seem

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:40.480
to be a link, consistent with
data showing about a doubling

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:42.000
of risk for developing dementia among

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.000
those acutely pesticide poisoned.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.920
Among U.S. elders, DDT and its
breakdown product DDE are also

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.520
associated with increased risk
of cognitive decline in general.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:54.080
DDT was extensively
used in the United States

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:56.000
from the 1940s through the 1960s.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.240
At its peak, we were churning
out 180 million pounds a year.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.880
And it’s still in our bodies to this day,

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:06.320
contaminating the bloodstreams of
more than 90 percent of Americans,

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:09.280
and DDE, the pesticide
linked to quadrupling the odds

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:11.920
of Alzheimer’s, were at the highest levels.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:15.200
It’s still in our bodies because
it’s still in the food supply.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:18.000
In my last video on the topic,
I noted that the levels of

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.200
DDT, DDE, and other banned
pesticides and pollutants

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.400
were much lower in the breast
milk from a vegetarian mother

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:27.600
compared to breast milk of
her non-vegetarian sister.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.160
And the largest difference
was noted for DDE,

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.800
which was four times lower
in the vegetarian sister.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.400
These toxins build up the food
chain; so, it makes sense that

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.880
the most contaminated foods are
meat, fish, and dairy products.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:44.480
5 to 10 times higher
levels in meat, eggs,

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.520
fish, and dairy than what
they found in plant foods.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:51.760
And unfortunately, cooking doesn’t
destroy pollutants like DDE—

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.400
in fact it may make them
even more concentrated.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:57.920
And this is for a pesticide
that may increase the risk

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:00.320
of Alzheimer’s disease as
much as if you carried

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:03.674
the so-called Alzheimer’s
gene APOE&nbsp;&nbsp;4.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:07.532
In our next story, we look
at the role of meat

00:04:07.532 --> 00:04:09.280
on Alzheimer’s disease.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:13.120
What is behind the dramatic
increase in dementia

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:14.960
in Japan over recent decades?

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.360
Maybe it’s rising obesity rates

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:20.000
or the increases in cholesterol,
saturated fat, and iron,

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.560
or increases in animal products and meat.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:27.600
Overall, calories just went
up about 10% in Japan,

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.360
whereas animal fat and meat
consumption rose 500%,

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:34.560
about 10 times the rise in sugary junk.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:38.720
Now, during this timespan,
rice consumption went down,

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.000
but the thinking is that

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.240
rather than white rice
somehow being protective,

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:45.040
maybe they were just eating
something worse instead.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:47.947
It’s like when you find fish consumption

00:04:47.947 --> 00:04:49.120
is correlated with less disease,

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:51.440
you wonder if it’s because
they’re eating that

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.040
rather than some worse meat.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:55.840
If you look across multiple countries,

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:57.120
you see a similar pattern,

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.360
with the most important
dietary link to Alzheimer’s

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:01.360
appearing to be meat consumption,

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:04.800
with eggs and high-fat
dairy also maybe contributing.

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:07.360
There appears to be a
really tight correlation

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:09.840
between Alzheimer’s and
per capita meat supply.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.840
And, then, studies within
countries uncover similar findings,

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:15.360
with Alzheimer’s and cognitive decline

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:19.920
associated with meaty,
sweety, fatty diets,

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.400
whereas most plant
foods were associated

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:23.840
with risk reduction.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:26.720
This could be for a variety of reasons.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:27.680
Animal products tend to have

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:29.840
more copper, mercury,
lead, and cadmium,

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:30.880
and no folate,

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:33.040
but contain saturated fat and cholesterol,

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:36.000
and pro-inflammatory advanced
glycation end products;

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:39.200
so many mechanisms
that dietary modification

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:40.800
may be our best bet

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:43.280
for reducing risk of
Alzheimer’s disease.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:46.960
But, how do we know
it’s cause-and-effect –

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:48.240
the evidence that meat consumption

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:51.280
is causally linked to Alzheimer’s disease?

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:52.960
Well, there’s the strength
of the association,

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:55.520
the consistency across
different types of studies,

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:57.680
the fact that the dietary changes

00:05:57.680 --> 00:05:59.280
preceded the risk of dementia,

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:00.320
the dose response—

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:02.480
more meat linked to more risk—

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:04.480
a bunch of plausible mechanisms.

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:06.080
We know that meat is a risk factor

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:07.280
for other chronic diseases,

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:10.080
but there’s never been
randomized controlled trial

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:11.440
to put it to the test.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:14.400
When you read reviews
of the damaging effects

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:17.680
of high-fat diets to
the brain and cognition,

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:19.280
a number of factors are proposed

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:20.960
to account for the high-fat diet-induced

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:22.320
damage to the brain.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:24.720
Oxidative stress, insulin
resistance, inflammation,

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:26.480
and changes to blood vessels

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:28.960
and the integrity of
the blood brain barrier.

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:32.640
But, these are based mostly
on studies of rodents.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:35.520
Yes, high fat diets can cause

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:36.960
energy dysfunction in the brain,

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:39.680
based on fancy MRI techniques.

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:42.240
With CT scans, you can follow this

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:44.320
intracranial artery stenosis,

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:46.720
this brain artery clogging, over time,

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:49.280
and follow the progression
from mild cognitive impairment

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:50.480
to Alzheimer’s disease.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:53.680
Those who only had
low-grade stenosis

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:55.280
were pretty stable over time

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:56.720
in terms of their cognitive function

00:06:57.360 --> 00:06:58.800
and ability to dress themselves,

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:00.240
and other activities of daily living,

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:02.240
whereas those with more clogging

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:04.480
started slipping over the years.

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:06.000
And those who started
out with the most

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.880
brain atherosclerosis
rapidly went downhill,

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:12.000
and twice as likely to progress

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:13.360
to full-blown Alzheimer’s.

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:16.880
Chronic consumption of
standard Western diets,

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:18.480
and rich in saturated
fat and cholesterol,

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:22.080
may compromise our
cerebrovascular integrity

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:24.240
compromise the blood
vessels in our brain.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:28.000
So, of course, drugs are recommended,

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:31.360
“pharmacological modulation”
of diet-induced dysfunction.

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:36.720
But why not just try to eat healthier
in the first place?

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:42.480
Finally today, we look at
reducing glycotoxin intake

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:44.220
to ward off Alzheimers.

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:48.880
Each of us has about 6 billion
miles of DNA.

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:52.720
How does our body keep it
from getting all tangled up?

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:55.360
There are special proteins
called histones,

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:58.320
which act like spools
with DNA as the thread.

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:02.400
Enzymes called sirtuins
wrap the DNA

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:04.000
around the histones spools

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:06.960
and in doing so silence
whatever genes

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:09.280
were in that
stretch of DNA,

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:10.640
hence their name sirtuins,

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:13.520
which stands for silencing
information regulator.

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:16.880
Although they were discovered
only about a decade ago,

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:18.560
the study of sirtuins
has become one

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:21.360
of the most promising
areas of biomedicine,

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:22.960
since they appear to
be involved

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:25.840
in promoting healthy
aging and longevity.

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:29.520
Suppression of this
key host defense

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:32.880
is considered a central feature
of Alzheimer’s disease.

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:38.800
Autopsies of Alzheimer’s victims
reveal that loss of sirtuin enzyme activity

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:41.040
is closely associated
with the accumulation

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:42.640
of the plaques and tangles
in the brain

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:45.040
that’s characteristic of
Alzheimer’s disease.

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:48.720
Sirtuins appear to
activate pathways

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:51.280
that steer the brain away
from the formation

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:53.200
of plaque and
tangle proteins.

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:55.680
Because a decrease in
sirtuin activity

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:58.960
can clearly have deleterious
effects on nerve health,

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:00.640
they’re trying to
come up with drugs

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:02.400
to increase sirtuin activity,

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:08.160
but why not just prevent its
suppression in the first place?

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:12.800
Glycotoxins in food
suppress sirtuin activity-

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:16.240
these so-called advanced
glycation end products, or AGE’s.

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:19.120
Our modern diet includes
excessive AGE’s,

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:21.200
which can be neurotoxic.

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:22.960
High levels in the blood

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:26.560
may predict cognitive
decline over time.

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:30.080
If you measure the urine
levels of glycotoxins

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:32.160
flowing through the bodies
of older adults,

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:33.840
those with the
highest levels

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:37.280
went on to suffer the
greatest cognitive decline

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:39.600
over the subsequent
nine years.

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:44.880
As we age, our brain
literally shrinks.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:49.520
In our 60's and 70's we lose
an average of five cubic centimeters

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:52.080
of total brain tissue
volume every year,

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:55.200
but some lose more
than others.

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:00.560
Brain atrophy may be reduced in
very healthy individuals,

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:03.840
and a few people don’t
lose any brain at all.

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:07.280
Normally we lose about 2% of
brain volume every year,

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:09.280
but that’s just
the average.

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:11.440
Although the average
brain loss for folks

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:13.120
in their 70's and 80's
was 2.1%,

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:17.040
some lost more,
some lost less,

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:20.000
and some men and women
lost none at all

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:21.600
over a period of
four years.

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:25.280
Researchers in Australia
provided the first evidence

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:29.120
linking AGEs with this kind
of cerebral brain loss.

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:32.480
So limiting one’s consumption
of these compounds

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:36.000
may end up having significant
public health benefits.

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:40.000
Because sirtuin deficiency
is both preventable

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:43.760
and reversible by dietary
AGE reduction,

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:47.360
a therapeutic strategy that
includes eating less AGE's

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:51.440
may offer a new strategy to combat
the epidemic of Alzheimer’s.

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:56.240
Some of these glycotoxins
are produced internally,

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:57.440
particularly in diabetics,

00:10:57.440 --> 00:11:00.400
but anyone can get them
from smoking and eating,

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:04.320
particularly foods high
in fat and protein.

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:12.080
In a previous video I listed the 15 foods
most contaminated with glycotoxins,

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:16.080
mostly chicken, but also
pork, beef, and fish,

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:19.200
which may help explain why
those that eat the most meat

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:22.160
may have triple the risk of
getting dementia

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:24.733
compared to long-time
vegetarians.

